oth parseability and normalized gain at or above threshold.\" So parseable must be True.\n\nReturn results.\n\nThe implementation must be a function solve(case) that returns a dict.\n\nWe need to produce JSON string with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source code as a JSON-escaped string.\n\nLet's write the Python code.\n\nWe should also handle case where 'calls' might be missing? But case is guaranteed to have those fields per fixture. Raise ValueError for structural issues.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    if 'calls' not in case or 'threshold' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call.get('call_id')\n        loss_without = call.get('loss_without_call')\n        loss_with = call.get('loss_with_call')\n        tokens = call.get('prediction_tokens')\n        parseable = call.get('parseable')\n        # Validate\n        if None in (call_id, loss_without, loss_with, tokens, parseable):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing fields in call {call_id}\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens if tokens != 0 else 0.0  # safeguard\n        accepted = bool(parseable) and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': accepted,\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\n        })\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_call_ids,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut note: the output schema requires normalized_gain to be number, not rounded. That's fine.\n\nAlso note: The instruction says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order.\" Those are not used? They are